WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
How often do you lie?

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.320
And how often do you think
other people lie to you?

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.800
Chances are, it's a lot more often
than you think.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:14.040
There are a number of different
reasons why we lie.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.760
So we might lie for personal gain,

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:20.680
we might lie to avoid punishment,

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.880
we might lie because we get a kick

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.440
out of pulling the wool over
somebody else's eyes.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.640
So the white lies, I always say,

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.480
are the oil that keeps life's
machinery running smoothly.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.120
Everybody lies.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.160
Imagine a situation where
we told the truth all of the time.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.280
I really don't think
we'd last very long,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.840
we certainly wouldn't have friends
for more than about 24 hours.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.200
People lie to get rewards
and avoid harm.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.800
So for example,
in order to get a good job

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:05.640
you might think
I would lie on my CV.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.600
You might lie to gain more money.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.440
People also lie often
because they want others

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:11.800
to see them more favourably.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.280
In order to get a better date

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.680
you might think, "I would
lie on a dating app."

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.120
According to George Lakoff
at the University of California,

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.560
we tend to interpret
evidence differently

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.440
depending on our personal beliefs.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.240
And anything that challenges that

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.040
will actually be ignored
or even attacked,

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.080
which might go some way to explaining
why society can be so polarised.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.440
Research suggests
that when we're married

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:29.240
about one in 10 of our
interactions is a form of deception.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:32.640
But when we're in the early stages
of getting to know a person,

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:33.880
or dating them,

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.080
it can be as high
as almost half the things

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.280
that we tell the other person could
be deceptive in some kind of way.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:45.040
The most common type of lies that
we tend to tell are the white lies,

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.680
the protective lies.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.800
So imagine your partner
is a budding Picasso,

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.920
they come home from
art class one night

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.920
and they show you their latest
painting that they're very proud of.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.760
You take a look at it and go, "Mm."

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.240
And they say, "What do you
think of my painting?"

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.840
We would typically go,
"Oh, yes, I quite like that."

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.280
We wouldn't necessarily
tell the outright truth

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.000
that we wouldn't have that
on the wall if you paid us to.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:16.840
When we learn to lie as children

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.520
we use something called
the theory of mind,

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.520
which is our understanding of the
intentions and beliefs of others.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.920
We also develop skills such
as planning and self-control

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.800
which help us tell better porkies.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.760
Robert Feldman at the
University of Massachusetts

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.240
found that we lie frequently and we
don't even know how often we do it.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.760
Participants in his study lied,
on average,

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.000
three times in a
10-minute conversation

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.960
when they were meeting
each other for the first time.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.960
They weren't aware that
they were lying that much

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.680
until they watched back
the footage of the interactions.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.680
We like to think of ourselves as very
honest, truthful, trustworthy people.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.600
So one of the reasons
that we might not be good

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.120
at tracking how often
that we've lied,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.960
we might be underestimating
quite a lot about how often we lie,

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.600
we might underestimate
to protect ourselves,

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.640
to protect our self-esteem, make
ourselves feel better about ourself.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.320
You might think it's OK to lie
in certain social situations,

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:19.120
I mean what's wrong with telling
someone that they look good today?

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.760
But are little acts of dishonesty
as harmless as they seem?

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.240
Is lying about small things
the start of a slippery slope?

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:30.440
There are many famous examples by
which people started with small lies

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:33.200
that seem to have expanded
into bigger and bigger lies.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.560
Bernie Madoff is one example
with his Ponzi scheme.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:38.840
He himself says that he
started with small lies,

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:43.640
but over time it became a really
big snowball that expanded.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.760
So when someone lies,
they often feel bad about it

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:50.600
because we think lies are immoral
and so if we lie we feel bad.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:54.760
But the thing is that when
we have an emotion

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:58.320
and then we encounter
the same stimulus again

00:04:58.400 --> 00:04:59.720
that triggered that emotion,

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.120
the amount of emotion
that we feel is reduced.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.920
If we allow people
to get away with small lies,

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:09.560
it is possible that over time

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:14.400
they will be more likely to
be more and more dishonest

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.680
and actually commit bigger crimes.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:20.160
This has some implication
for education for example,

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.800
trying to stop kids even
when they tell little lies,

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.080
but also for law enforcement.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.800
If we stopped small
acts of dishonesty

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:30.840
that could, potentially, over time,

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:35.080
make it less likely that people will
commit large acts of dishonesty.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:40.160
Thanks for watching. Don't forget to subscribe

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:43.020
and click the bell to receive notifications for new videos.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:44.420
See you again soon!

